{"tool_name":"keggtranslator","contents":["oeb","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"keggtranslator","home":"http://www.ra.cs.uni-tuebingen.de/software/KEGGtranslator/index.htm","summary":"Easy-to-use stand-alone application that can visualize and convert KGML formatted XML-files into multiple output formats.","addition_date":"2017-08-03T18:47:32Z","last_update_date":"2018-12-10T12:58:56Z","tool_type":["Desktop application"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:keggtranslator","credit":[{"typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"http://www.zbit.uni-tuebingen.de"}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"http://www.ra.cs.uni-tuebingen.de/software/KEGGtranslator/doc/index.html"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Formatting","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0335"}]}],"name":"KEGGtranslator","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"mbs_import","publication":[{"metadata":{"abstract":"The KEGG PATHWAY database provides a widely used service for metabolic and nonmetabolic pathways. It contains manually drawn pathway maps with information about the genes, reactions and relations contained therein. To store these pathways, KEGG uses KGML, a proprietary XML-format. Parsers and translators are needed to process the pathway maps for usage in other applications and algorithms. We have developed KEGGtranslator, an easy-to-use stand-alone application that can visualize and convert KGML formatted XML-files into multiple output formats. Unlike other translators, KEGGtranslator supports a plethora of output formats, is able to augment the information in translated documents (e.g. MIRIAM annotations) beyond the scope of the KGML document, and amends missing components to fragmentary reactions within the pathway to allow simulations on those. \u00a9 The Author(s) 2011. Published by Oxford University Press.","authors":[{"name":"Drager A."},{"name":"Wrzodek C."},{"name":"Zell A."}],"citationCount":41